    "content" : "3.1 Zero Infimum Spacing"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 2,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 600.489, 477.482, 646.318 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The next theorem gives a condition under which the infimum spacing is zero. Here our main result is Theorem 2, where we show that the existence of a nonzero repulsive fixed point of the spectral decimation function with multiplier, larger than the zero fixed point indicates zero infimum spacing in the spectrum."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 549.581, 477.48, 590.527 ],
    "font" : "CMTI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Theorem 2. Assume ∆ admits spectral decimation with spectral decimation function R and suppose 0,ζ > 0 are fixed point of R with |R′(ζ)| > R′(0) > 1. Then inf{|λ − λ′| : λ ̸= λ′,λ,λ′ ∈ σ(∆)} = 0."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 500.863, 477.478, 546.692 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof. Let ϕζ be the inverse branch of R with ζ in it’s range. Note that ζ is in the Julia set of R and is an attracting fixed point of ϕζ and hence we may choose n, and x1,x2 ∈ σ(∆n) so that ϕmζ (xj) → ζ,j = 1,2 [HSTZ11]. Therefore, we have,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 182.464, 469.437, 361.618, 488.568 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "ϕmζ (x1) − ϕmζ (x2) = (ϕmζ (γm))′ |x1 − x2|"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 263.56, 447.519, 430.444, 466.799 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "= ϕ′ζ(ϕmζ −1(γm)) ... ϕ′ζ(γm) |x1 − x2|"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 411.211, 477.479, 440.202 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Note that ϕζ is a continuous injective map and hence monotone. Therefore, it is no loss to assume, ϕkζ(x1) ≤ ϕkζ(γm) ≤ ϕkζ(x2),∀k."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 375.651, 477.484, 413.213 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Thus, ϕkζ(γm) − ζ ≤ maxj∈{1,2} ϕkζ(xj) − ζ Hence, by continuity of R′, given δ > 0 there is N so that m ≥ k ≥ N =⇒ R′(ζ) − δ ≤ R′(ϕkζ(γm) . Thus,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 136.398, 359.541, 177.529, 378.478 ],
    "font" : "CMR7",
    "font size" : 6.974,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1 R′(ϕkζ(γm))"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
